course: dictionary
lesson: 7905
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The meaning that fits depends on the context, which means the words and sentences around the word. «Raspberry» is a good example. Look at the clues, then decide which meaning fits.
explain: Here the word is a fruit: «A girl in Oregon ate bright red berries in a forest because they looked like raspberries.» These are small red fruit on a bush. But the dictionary also gives a rude noise, so always check the sentence before you decide.
explain: Capital letters and nearby clues can change the meaning. «The Raspberry Pi, a cheap credit-card-sized computer made in the UK,» is a computer, not a fruit. Whatever meaning you pick, swap it into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A raspberry is a small, soft, red fruit made of tiny round parts, and it grows on a bush.
fact: To blow a raspberry means to stick out your tongue and blow, making a rude noise; in the US it is called a «Bronx cheer».
fact: Raspberries are a summer fruit, good in «raspberry jam» and desserts.
fact: Botanically, raspberries are not true berries: «neither are raspberries and blackberries.»
fact: The Raspberry Pi is a cheap, credit-card-sized computer, launched in 2012 to help people learn coding.
exercise: In «The Raspberry Pi, a cheap credit-card-sized computer made in the UK, helps people learn coding», what is the raspberry? | A small red fruit that grows on a bush | A rude noise | A kind of jam | *A cheap computer
exercise: Jamal says, «Stop it, don't blow a raspberry at me!» What does raspberry mean here? | A type of bush | A computer game | *A rude noise made by sticking out your tongue and blowing | A small red fruit
exercise: Find and copy the word from «The Raspberry Pi is a cheap, credit-card-sized computer» that is a clue it is a machine, not a fruit. | cheap | learn | *computer | launched
test: «Her little brother blew a raspberry at the broccoli.» Which meaning of raspberry fits? | *A rude noise, made by sticking out your tongue and blowing | A small red fruit that grows on a bush | A cheap computer | A summer dessert
test: According to the text, what are raspberries, botanically? | *Not true berries | Fruit with its seeds on the outside, like a strawberry | A kind of computer | True berries, like grapes
exam: When a word has more than one meaning, what should you do? | *Read the words around it and test each meaning in the sentence | Ignore the sentence and guess | Look only at the capital letters | Pick the first meaning you ever learned
